KEYWORDS: DNAzyme; metal-organic frameworks; apoptosis; $Z n^{2+}$ ; phosphate ion; two-photon imaging
Apoptosis is a programmed process of cell death that occurs during normal development and homeostasis, and plays indispensable roles in immunologic defense, embryo development, and the elimination of excess cells.1, 2 Dysregulated apoptosis induces multifarious diseases, such as neurodegenerative diseases and autoimmune diseases.3, 4 The zinc ion $\left(\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}\right)$ is known to be an important factor in apoptosis $^{2,~5}$ and the depletion of cellular $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ enhances the rate of apoptosis and cell death.6 Moreover, the release of $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ from intracellular depots or metalloproteins occurs in the early process of apoptosis.7 Many biological molecules and exogenous compounds can induce apoptosis, including tumor necrosis factors and anticancer drugs.6 Hence, the highly accurate detection of intracellular endogenous $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ is of great significance in the study of apoptosis-related cell biology and disease diagnosis.
To date, various fluorescent sensors for analyzing $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ in living cells have been reported.8-10 However, most of the reported fluorescent probes are based on organic small molecules that generally respond to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ at the ratio of 1:1, which restricts their sensitivity.11-13 Since their discovery by the Joyce group,14 RNA-cleaving, DNAzyme-based biosensors with high target selectivity have attracted intense interest.15-18 Different nanocarriers including gold nanoparticles,19 graphene oxide,20 and up-conversion nanoparticles 21 have been used to deliver DNAzyme into cells for imaging intracellular $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ . However, most of the intracellular sensing efforts using DNAzyme have suffered from several drawbacks that remain to be addressed: (1) Most of the current DNAzyme-based biosensors are excited by single photons at short wavelengths, resulting in shallow tissue penetration $\scriptstyle\left(<\mathbf{100}\ \upmu\mathbf{m}\right)$ along with high background interference. $^{22,23}\left(2\right)$ Their poor cellular uptake efficiency and susceptibility toward enzymatic degradation hinder intracellular applications.24 $\left(3\right)$ DNAzyme cannot reach the desired locations in cells before being “turned on”.25, 26 Therefore, developing new DNAzyme-based probes that conquer these drawbacks is in high demand, particularly for the study of apoptosis.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed using metal ions/clusters and organic linkers have attracted increasing attention in the fields of biosensing,27 drug delivery28 and therapy,29 due to their high porosity, large surface area, and tunable metal ions/clusters and ligands. $30^{-}32$ Among the various types of MOFs, UiO-66 containing $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters as the metal nodes with terephthalic acid as the bridging ligand has been widely used to load nucleic acid molecules. The Zr atoms of UiO66 strongly coordinate with the phosphonate O atoms, and the release of the modified nucleic acid molecules loaded onto UiO-66 is triggered by the higher concentration of phosphate ions inside living cells.28, 33 Therefore, UiO-66 could potentially be employed as a nanocarrier to release active DNAzyme for specific $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ sensing in living cells. Further, since most of the reported probes for $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ imaging was realized by single-photon imaging with low tissue penetration, more efficient probes with deeper tissue penetration are highly desired, along with lower autofluorescence background and low cytotoxicity.
Here, using UiO-66 as the nanocarrier, we developed and successfully applied a DNAzyme-MOFs-based twophoton (TP) nanoprobe for the detection of apoptosisassociated $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ in live/apoptotic cells and deep tissues. As shown in Scheme 1, the substrate-binding strand of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ -specific DNAzyme (8-17) was labeled with a TP fluorophore at the $5^{\prime}$ end and the enzyme strand was labeled with a quencher, $\mathrm{BHQ_{1}}$ , at the $3^{'}$ end. The two strands were first mixed together to form the doublestranded DNAzyme structure, resulting in fluorescence quenching of the TP fluorophore through the proximity of BHQ1.
Scheme 1. Schematic illustration of the construction of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs nanoprobe and its application in monitoring cell apoptosis.
Then, the double-stranded molecules (TP-DNAzyme) were loaded onto the surface of the MOFs by the spontaneous multivalent coordination between the DNA backbone phosphate and the unsaturated zirconium sites on the MOFs. After entering the cells, the TP-DNAzyme was released from the MOFs because of the higher concentration of phosphate ions inside the cells than that in the extracellular environment. In the presence of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , the TP-labeled substrate strand was subsequently cleaved and a short DNA strand containing the TP fluorophore was thereby released from the TP-DNAzyme, which resulted in the fluorescence signal being “turned on.”
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials and apparatus. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(NO_{3})_{2}\bullet6H_{2}O,\ g9\%]}}$ , zirconium tetrachloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4:}}$ $98\%$ , pyrithione ( $40\%$ in water), terephthalic acid $(99\%)$ , and N, N, N ′ , $\mathrm{~\bf~N~}^{\prime}$ ′ -tetrakis-(2-pyridylmethyl)- ethylenediamine (TPEN) were purchased from the Energy Chemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). The Cell Counting Kit-8 (CCK-8) and the Annexin V Apoptosis Detection KitFITC/PI was obtained from the Sangon Biotech Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Dimethylformamide $(99.5\%)$ , ethanol $(99.0\%)$ , and glacial acetic acid $(99.5\%)$ ) were provided by the Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). The TP-labeled substrate strand of the DNAzyme: 5 ′ -TP-ACTCACTAT/rA/GGAAGAGATGAGGCCC, and the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ -activated enzyme strand of the DNAzyme: $5^{^{\prime}}$ ′- GGGCCTCATCTCTTCTCCGAGCCGGTCGAAATAGTGAG T-BHQ1-3 ′ , were synthesized by Takara Biomedical Technology Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China). The ultrapure water was prepared with a Millipore Milli-Q water purification system (Billerica, MA, USA). All reagents were used as supplied without further purification.
The fluorescence spectrum was obtained with a Hitachi F-4600 fluorescence spectrophotometer (Hitachi, Japan). The excitation and emission slit width were 5.0 nm and 10.0 nm, respectively, and the photomultiplier tube (PMT) voltage was set at 700 V. NMR spectra were realized with an AV Ⅲ HD 600 MHz NMR spectrometer (Bruker, Switzerland). The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were obtained with the Sigma 500 field emission scanning electron microscope (Carl Zeiss, UK). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained with a FEI Tecnai G2 microscope (FEI, Hillsboro, OR, USA). The X-Ray diffraction (XRD) analysis was realized with an X ′ Pert PRO X-ray diffractometer (PANalytical, Almelo, Netherlands). The zeta potential and diameter distribution were obtained with a NanoPlus- $^3$ zeta potential/nanoparticle size analyzer (Micromeritics, Norcross, GA, USA). The concentration of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}^{4+}$ was quantified by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry with an OptiMass 9500/NWR-213 (Romquest Technologies Corp., Melbourne, Australia). The cellular viability was determined with a Multiskan GO microplate reader (Thermo Scientific, Vantaa, Finland). Two-photon fluorescence images of HeLa cells and liver tissues were acquired with a TCS SP8 confocal laser scanning microscope (Leica, Wetzlar, Germany).
Synthesis of MOFs. The UiO-66 MOFs was used to protect and deliver the TP-DNAzyme nanoprobes. The MOFs were synthesized according to a previously reported method.31 Briefly, $67.2\ \mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $48{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of 1, 4- benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ ; $99\%$ ) were each dispersed in $12~\mathrm{mL}$ dimethylformamide (DMF). The two solutions were mixed together in a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ round-bottom flask, another $\mathbf{16}~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF was added, and $2\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid was dropped into the solution with stirring. Next, the mixture was incubated in an oil bath at $90^{\circ}C$ for 12 h. When the temperature of the solution subsided below $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the obtained solid was washed with ethanol three times. The resulting white powder was dried under vacuum at ambient temperature. The final prepared MOFs were dispersed in ultrapure water for subsequent studies.
Synthesis of TP-DNAzyme-MOFs nanoprobe. TPDNAzyme-MOFs were synthesized by binding TPDNAzyme to the surface of the MOFs through spontaneous multivalent coordination. Briefly, 100 nM of TP-DNAzyme was mixed with 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ of MOFs solution and incubated at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{\scriptsize1h}}$ . This mixture was washed three times by Tris buffer. The final TP-DNAzyme-MOFs preparation was resuspended in Tris buffer and stored at 4 $^{\circ}C$ .
Measurement of the loading ability of the MOFs. First, 100 nM of TP-DNAzyme (without $\mathrm{BHQ_{1}})$ and 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ of MOFs were mixed together for 1 h at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This mixture was centrifuged at ${5,000\times\mathrm{g}}$ for 15 min and then the supernatant was collected to measure its fluorescence intensity (termed $^{\omega}\mathrm{F}_{1}^{\eta\prime}.$ ). The fluorescence intensity of 100 $\mathbf{nM}$ free TP-DNAzyme was also determined (termed $^{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{F_{\mathrm{o}}}^{\mathfrak{n}}$ ). The loading efficiency of the TP-DNAzyme on the MOFs was then calculated using the following equation: $\left(\mathrm{F_{o}{-}F_{\mathrm{1}}}\right)/\mathrm{F_{o}}$ .
Polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis. A 12 $\%$ polyacrylamide gel was used to determine the stability of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs in the presence of bacteriophage $\lambda$ exonuclease $(\lambda_{\mathrm{exo}})$ and to visualize the release of the DNAzymes from the MOFs. Electrophoresis was implemented in $\mathbf{\sigma}_{1\times}$ TBE buffer (90 mM Tris-HCl, 90 mM boric acid, $_{2\mathrm{mM}}$ EDTA, $\mathrm{pH}8.0\AA\AA$ ) at 80 V constant voltage for 1 h at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Next, SYBR Gold was used to stain the gel and the gel image was captured with a Bio-Rad fluorescence gel imaging system (Bio-Rad Laboratories, USA).
In vitro fluorescence measurement. First, 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe was incubated in phosphatebuffered saline (10 mM PBS, $\mathrm{pH}=7.4,$ ) for $_4\mathrm{~h~}$ . Solutions containing different concentrations of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ $\scriptstyle(0-20\upmu\mathrm{M})$ were then added, followed by further incubation for 20 min. The single-photon fluorescence emission spectrum of the TP dye was collected over 480–610 nm with the excitation wavelength of 400 nm. Using the same method, the selectivity of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs was tested against other metal ions with a final concentration of $25~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ , including $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Fe}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}^{4+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ , $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ , $\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ .
Cell viability test. The CCK-8 kit was used to determine the effect of the MOFs and TP-DNAzyme-MOFs on cell viability. HeLa cells $(7.4\times10^{3}$ cells) in 100 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Dulbecco’s Minimal Eagle’s Medium (DMEM) containing $10\%$ fetal bovine serum (FBS), penicillin (100 $\mathrm{U/mL}$ ), and streptomycin (100 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL},$ ) were first seeded into 96-well microliter plates. After the cells were incubated with $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $_{24}\mathrm{h}$ , a series of concentration (0, 10, 25, 50, 75, 100, and 200 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL})$ of the MOFs or TP-DNAzymeMOFs were incubated with the HeLa cells for 2 h and $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. The medium was then removed, the cells were washed with PBS buffer, and the cells were cultured in fresh culture medium of DMEM for $4^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . Next, 10 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of CCK-8 was added to the medium and the cells were incubated for a further $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The absorbance at 490 nm was captured with a microplate reader.
Two-photon imaging of live/apoptotic cells. The potential of the obtained TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe for TP imaging was tested with HeLa cells. First, the HeLa cells were incubated overnight in DMEM at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . After treatment with 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ of the TP-DNAzymeMOFs probe for $_{3}\mathrm{~h~}$ , the cells were cultured with a concentration series (0, 10, 20, and $50~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ and pyrithione at a molar ratio of 1:2 for 40 min and then imaged with a CLSM using an excitation wavelength of 760 nm over the emission wavelength range of $500{-}600~\mathrm{nm}$ .
TP imaging was carried out in a similar way to study cell apoptosis by monitoring the changing of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ levels in HeLa cells. Apoptosis of the HeLa cells was confirmed with the Annexin V Apoptosis Detection Kit-FITC/PI. Briefly, the HeLa cells were pretreated with ${250\upmu\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}}$ for $_{3}\mathrm{h}$ to induce apoptosis, which produces high levels of endogenous $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}.$ .7 The cells were then treated with 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe for $_{3}\mathrm{~h~}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , then treated with ${\bf\mathrm{~\bf~2~}}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the Annexin $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ Apoptosis Detection Kit-FITC/PI reagent for another 10 min. For the control, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -pretreated cells were first cultured for 30 min with $50~\mathrm{mM}$ of the zinc chelator, TPEN, before the addition of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe and the apoptosis detection reagent as above. Finally, the cells were imaged with a CLSM using the excitation wavelengths of $760\mathrm{nm}$ and $552\ensuremath{\mathrm{nm}}$ over the emission wavelength ranges of 500–600 nm and 600–700 nm, respectively.
Two-photon imaging of tissues. To acquire TP images of apoptosis in tissue, rat liver slices were used. The tissue samples were treated with 100 $\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mL}$ of the TP-DNAzymeMOFs for $_{3}\mathrm{~h~}$ and 100 $\upmu\mathrm{M}\textrm Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ for 40 min. Subsequently, the tissue slices were imaged by CLSM using a modelocked titanium laser and an excitation wavelength fixed at $760\mathrm{nm}$ .
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Synthesis and characterization of TP-DNAzymeMOFs. As SEM indicated (Figure 1A), the prepared MOFs displayed uniform, monodispersed, and regular octahedron morphology, which was further validated by TEM (Figure S9). The XRD analysis indicated that the MOFs contained specific peaks consistent with a standard MOFs crystal structure (Figure 1C). The size distribution of the MOFs showed an average hydrodynamic diameter of $201.6\pm3.8~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S10). The zeta potential indicated that the surface of the MOFs was positively charged, which was likely the result of the unsaturated zirconium sites on the MOFs (Figure 1D).
To verify the absorption capacity of the MOFs toward DNA, the MOFs were mixed with TP-DNAzyme formed from a two-photon fluorophore-labeled substrate strand and an enzyme strand. The zeta potential of the MOFs changed from positive to negative after the addition of the DNA, which implies that the DNA had attached on the surface of the MOFs (Figure 1D). Compared with the average hydrodynamic diameter of the MOFs, the diameter of TP-DNAzyme-MOFs is increased to ${296.6\pm}$
17.4 nm (Figure S10), which was congruous with the presence of DNA on the surface of the MOFs. The XRD analysis demonstrated that the loading of the DNA had little influence on the crystal structure of the MOFs (Figure 1C). The loading efficiency of MOFs was investigated by determining the fluorescence intensity of the TP-labeled DNAzyme before and after incubation with UiO-66 MOFs. The loading efficiency was calculated to be $69.5\%$ (Figure S11), corresponding to the adsorption of about $4{\cdot}18\times10^{11}$ TP-DNAzyme per 1 μg of MOFs.
To investigate the stability of the nanoprobe, fluorescence spectrum analysis, gel electrophoresis, DLS and Zeta potential test as well as the TEM characterization were carried out. UiO-66 nanoparticles have been shown to protect DNA from $\lambda_{\mathrm{exo}}$ degradation because of the steric hindrance on their surfaces. As expected, the fluorescence signal of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs showed a negligible increase after incubation with $\lambda_{\mathrm{exo}}$ ; however, a significant increase in fluorescence was observed for free TPDNAzyme after the same treatment (Figure 2A). These results were further validated by gel electrophoresis. As shown in Figure $\scriptstyle2\mathrm{B}$ , the DNAzyme-MOFs band was clearly visible and showed little change after incubation with $\lambda_{\mathrm{exo}},$ while the free DNAzyme was completely degraded under the same conditions. Moreover, both DLS and Zeta potential assays were also carried out on $\lambda_{\mathrm{exo}}$ -treated MOFs and $\lambda_{\mathrm{exo}}$ -treated TP-DNAzyme-MOFs, respectively. And the results show that $\lambda_{\mathrm{exo}}$ has negligible effect on both DLS and Zeta potential of MOFs and TP-DNAzyme-MOFs (Figure S12). All these results demonstrated that the nanoprobe possess the ability of resistance to enzyme digestion. Furthermore, the stability of MOFs was also studied under different temperatures. As shown in Figure S13, the morphology of the MOFs has no obvious change with the temperature increasing form $25^{\circ}C-45^{\circ}C$ .
Next, we verified the intracellular release of the TPDNAzymes from the nanoprobes following competition with the intracellular phosphate ions for coordination with the MOFs. Because of the high chelating capacity of phosphate ions and $Z\mathbf{r}$ , UiO-66 possesses the special property of intracellular phosphate-sensitive degradation. The SEM (Figure 1B) result showed that the diameter of the MOFs treated with 10 mM PBS for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ was clearly reduced and the morphology was also changed. In addition, the morphology of MOF in 10 mM PBS buffer with different pH (ranging from 6.0 to 8.0) were also recorded and the results show that the pH has no obvious effect on the degradation of MOFs (Figure S14). This reduction in the hydrodynamic diameter of both the MOFs and TP-DNAzyme-MOFs was consistent with the phosphate-sensitive property of the MOFs (Figure S15).
Furthermore, the phosphate-sensitive property of the MOFs was verified by measuring the content of $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ in the supernatant by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry. The concentration of $\mathrm{Zr^{4^{+}}}$ in the supernatant of the MOFs incubated in $_{2\mathrm{\mM}}$ PBS for 12 h showed no evident increase, while an evident increase was observed in that of the MOFs incubated in 10 mM PBS (Table S1). Additionally, both fluorescence signal assay (Figure $3\mathrm{A}$ and Figure S16) and gel electrophoresis (Figure $3\mathrm{B}_{,}^{\cdot}$ ) showed that the double-stranded DNAzyme were successfully released from the PBS-treated MOFs and the release behavior was highly phosphate ions concentration dependent. However, both pH and temperature have no obvious effect on the release of TP-DNAzyme from MOFs (Figure S17 and Figure S18). All these results convincingly indicated that the TPDNAzyme-MOFs probe was sensitive to the intracellular release of the double-stranded TP-DNAzyme. Owing to the low concentration of phosphate ions in extracellular fluids (approximately $_{2\mathrm{~mM}}$ ), the premature release of TPDNAzyme was prevented.37, 38 When TP-DNAzyme-MOFs was internalized into the cells, the on-demand release of TP-DNAzyme was effectively realized by the high concentration of phosphate inside the cells (approximately 10 mM).
$\mathbf{Z_{n^{2+}}}$ sensing in vitro. To attain optimal performance of the nanoprobe, the ratio of the TP-Zn-S strand to the BHQ1-Zn-E strand, the duration of the hybridization reaction between these strands, as well as the reaction time between the double-stranded DNAzyme molecule and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , were optimized. The results showed that a strand ratio of 1:2, with hybridization for 5 min and RNA cleavage for 20 min, achieved the highest response in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ detection (Figure S19).
To determine the sensing capacity of the TP-DNAzymeMOFs probe under conditions similar to that of the high intracellular phosphate concentration, the one-photon fluorescence assay was conducted. The PBS bufferselective sensing of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ by the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs was verified by comparing the fluorescence response in PBS buffer with that in Tris buffer. As shown in Figure 4A, an obvious fluorescence enhancement was observed in PBS buffer because the release of TP-DNAzyme from the MOFs resulted in cleavage of the substrate strand, producing a shorter, TP-labeled DNA strand.
Inset: linear fitting of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ detection in the concentration range of 5–500 nM. (D) Selectivity of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe toward $25~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ different metal ions. Data represent the mean $\pm$ standard deviation $\left(\mathbf{n}=3\right)$ .
Intracellular application of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs. Our results showed that the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs possess the capacity to detect $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ in vitro with satisfactory sensitivity and selectivity. Therefore, we next investigated the feasibility of using this biosensor to image $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ in live/apoptotic cells. The cytotoxicity of the TP-DNAzymeMOFs was first studied with the CCK-8 assay. HeLa cells were treated with a series of concentration of MOFs and TP-DNAzyme-MOFs for 2 h and $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively. The cell viability of all of the samples was higher than $88\%$ (Figure S20), indicating the negligible toxicity and outstanding biocompatibility of the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs biosensor.
Next, two-photon fluorescence imaging with the TPDNAzyme-MOFs in live cells was investigated. Under twophoton excitation, the cells incubated with the TPDNAzyme-MOFs probe only showed weak green fluorescence, indicating a low level of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in the live cells (Figure 5). Then, a series of $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ concentrations was incubated with the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe-treated cells and increasing green fluorescence was observed with increasing $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ concentration in the cytoplasm. The increments in the relative pixel intensity verified the fluorescence increase in the HeLa cells.
To further investigate the uptake of TP-DNAzymeMOFs by HeLa cells, $\mathrm{3D}$ scanning experiment was carried out. Only the middle of cell displayed fluorescence derived from TP-DNAzyme-MOFs in Figure S21. In order to validate that the fluorescence response was because of probe activity instead of interference in the cells, TPEN was applied as a membrane-permeable $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ chelating reagent to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ -pretreated cells before incubation with the TPDNAzyme-MOFs probe. As can be seen in Figure S22, while $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ -pretreated cells without TPEN treatment showed strong green fluorescence of the TP probe, the TPEN-treated cells exhibited much weaker fluorescence. Thus, all these results indicated that TP-DNAzyme-MOFs successfully entered cells and the fluorescent response arose from the activity of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ -specific DNAzyme.
On account of the reported release of $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ from intracellular metalloproteins during apoptosis,7 the TPDNAzyme-MOFs probe was further applied to monitor the process of apoptosis. To induce apoptosis, HeLa cells were first cultured with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for $_{3}\mathrm{h}$ . Afterward, the pretreated cells were incubated with the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe for $_{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -treated cells displayed strong red fluorescence when stained with the Annexin V Apoptosis Detection Kit-FITC/PI reagent, which demonstrated the presence of apoptosis (Figure 6). The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -treated cells exhibited stronger green fluorescence when treated with the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs probe than the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -untreated cells, attesting the increase in exogenous $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ during the process of cell apoptosis. To verify whether the fluorescent signal was derived from the response of the biosensor to the extricated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ during apoptosis, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -stimulated cells were further treated with the zinc inhibitor, TPEN, before treatment with the TP-DNAzyme-MOFs nanoprobe. The TPEN treatment led to a marked decrease in fluorescence intensity. Taken together, the results indicated that this probe could indeed be used to monitor apoptosis through imaging of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ .
