Two-photon bioimaging of $\mathbf{Z_{n^{2+}}}$ in tissue. In comparison with conventional one-photon imaging, a major advantage of a nanoprobe with a NIR excitation wavelength is that it can achieve deeper tissue imaging. Accordingly, the TP- DNAzyme-MOFs nanoprobe was applied to deep-tissue two-photon imaging of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ in rat liver tissue sections. As shown in Figure 7, the TPDNAzyme-MOFs probe allowed the effective fluorescence imaging of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ in tissue depths from 20 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to as deep as 120 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . These results indicate that the devised assay could be used for the imaging of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}}$ relatively deep within the tissue.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, a phosphate ion-activated DNAzyme-MOFsbased two-photon nanoprobe was developed to specifically monitor apoptosis through the imaging of intracellular $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ . In this assay, the two-photon dye-labeled DNAzyme is assembled onto MOFs and delivered into cells. The attached TP-DNAzyme is released from the internalized nanoprobe through displacement on the MOFs surface by intracellular inorganic phosphate. In the presence of the detection target, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ , the substrate strand of TPDNAzyme is cleaved to release a shorter DNA strand labeled with the TP fluorophore, leading to a marked increase in fluorescence that can be distinctly visualized with TP imaging. This TP-DNAzyme-MOFs biosensor allows the highly selective sensing of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}}$ , and the capacity for TP imaging facilitates the monitoring of apoptosisassociated $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ elevation in live cells and deep tissues up to 120 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . This phosphate ion-activation strategy offers great potential for various of medical applications.
